>
  <p>A board exam study timetable turns “I have to finish everything” into a plan for each day between now and your first paper. This guide works for CBSE, ICSE/ISC and state boards in Class 10 and Class 12. Check your own board’s date sheet and syllabus; the method stays the same.</p>

  <h2 id="phases">The 3-phase board exam plan</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Phase</th><th scope="col">Share of time</th><th scope="col">Goal</th><th scope="col">What you do</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>1. Complete</strong></td><td>~50%</td><td>Every chapter studied once</td><td>Textbook first, then textbook exercises. Short self-quiz after each topic.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>2. Revise</strong></td><td>~30%</td><td>Every chapter revised twice</td><td>Notes, formula sheets, flashcards, previous-year questions by chapter.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>3. Test</strong></td><td>~20%</td><td>Exam speed and accuracy</td><td>Full timed sample papers, checking against the marking scheme, fixing mistakes.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Why this order works: spreading revision across weeks gives much better long-term memory than revising once near the end (Cepeda et al., 2008), and practice testing is one of the two most effective study techniques identified in a major research review (Dunlosky et al., 2013). Sample papers are not just a final check; they are a learning tool.</p>

  <h2 id="months">Month-by-month plan (4 months out)</h2>
  <h3>Month 1: finish the syllabus, part 1</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>List every chapter for every subject from the official syllabus. Mark each red (weak), amber (okay) or green (strong).</li>
    <li>Finish pending chapters, starting with high-weightage reds.</li>
    <li>Sunday: 1-hour review of the week’s chapters without notes.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Month 2: finish the syllabus, part 2 + revision round 1 begins</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Complete all remaining chapters.</li>
    <li>Start revision round 1 for the chapters finished in month 1 (spaced review).</li>
    <li>Make one-page summary sheets per chapter: formulas, definitions, diagrams, dates.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Month 3: revision rounds 1 and 2</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Finish round 1 for all chapters, then begin round 2, faster, focused on reds and ambers.</li>
    <li>Solve previous-year questions chapter by chapter.</li>
    <li>One full sample paper per subject by the end of the month.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Month 4: sample papers and weak spots</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Two to three timed papers a week per major subject, written in exam conditions.</li>
    <li>Mark with the official marking scheme; add every mistake to a mistakes list.</li>
    <li>Short daily reviews of summary sheets and the mistakes list.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Shorter time? With 2 months, compress phases 1–2 into the first 5 weeks, prioritising high-weightage chapters, and keep the last 3 weeks for papers. The <a href="/tools/study-plan-generator/">study plan generator</a> builds a dated plan for any number of days.</p>

  <h2 id="daily">Daily timetables you can copy</h2>
  <h3>School day (about 3 focused hours)</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Time</th><th scope="col">Block</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>6:00–6:45</td><td>Formula/flashcard review (25 min) + quick self-quiz</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4:30–5:30</td><td>School homework and today’s topics</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6:30–7:30</td><td>Subject A: new chapter or revision, end with 10-min recall</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8:15–9:15</td><td>Subject B: practice questions</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10:30</td><td>Sleep</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <h3>Holiday / study leave (about 6.5 focused hours)</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Time</th><th scope="col">Block</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>7:30–9:00</td><td>Hardest subject: new topics or weakest chapter</td></tr>
      <tr><td>9:15–10:30</td><td>Same subject: practice questions</td></tr>
      <tr><td>11:00–12:30</td><td>Second subject</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2:30–4:30</td><td>Timed sample paper (or two sections), then check</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5:30–6:30</td><td>Third subject: revision from summary sheets</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8:00–8:45</td><td>Mistakes list + flashcards; plan tomorrow</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10:30</td><td>Sleep</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Keep the same bedtime every night. In a study of college students, consistent, good-quality sleep over weeks was linked to noticeably better test results; a single good night before the test was not enough (Okano et al., 2019).</p>

  <h2 id="subjects">How to split time between subjects</h2>
  <p>Give each subject a weight from <strong>how weak you are</strong> and <strong>how many marks are at stake</strong>:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Subject type</th><th scope="col">Blocks per week (example)</th><th scope="col">Best activity</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Maths / problem-heavy (weak)</td><td>6–7</td><td>Mixed practice sets, not one type at a time</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Science (Physics, Chemistry, Biology)</td><td>5–6</td><td>Concepts + numericals + diagram practice</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Social science / theory-heavy</td><td>4–5</td><td>Flashcards, timelines, answer writing from memory</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Languages</td><td>2–3</td><td>Writing practice, grammar sets, literature summaries</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>For maths, shuffle question types in a session rather than doing twenty of the same kind. In one study, students who practised mixed problems scored much higher on a later test than those who practised one type at a time (Rohrer &amp; Taylor, 2007).</p>

  <h2 id="final">The last 15 days and exam gaps</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No new chapters</strong> unless they carry high marks and you can finish them in a day.</li>
    <li><strong>One full paper a day</strong> for the subjects coming first, then fix every mistake.</li>
    <li><strong>Gaps between papers:</strong> spend them almost entirely on the next paper. Day 1: summary sheets and weak chapters. Day 2: one timed paper. Day before: mistakes list, formulas, early night.</li>
    <li><strong>Exam morning:</strong> a 20-minute look at your summary sheet only. No new material.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Put every paper into the <a href="/tools/exam-countdown/">exam countdown</a> to see your real study days and hours per day. In the ClassTrack app you can add each paper as an exam, get a countdown on your home screen, and ask the AI assistant for a plan for each gap.</p>
</Guide>
